    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kathmandu grew from two older settlements, Yambu and Yengal, which tradition says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunakamadeva">Gunakamadeva</a> joined into one city, and it prospered on the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</li><li>In the Malla period it was one of four fortified cities of the valley, until the Gorkha ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> took it in 1768 and made it the capital of the state that came to be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</li><li>Rana prime ministers governed from Kathmandu from 1846 to 1950, the monarchy was abolished there in 2008, and the earthquake of 2015 severely damaged its old squares.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Legend says Kathmandu's valley was once a deep lake, drained when the bodhisattva Manjushri cut the mountain with his sword, and the old city was laid out in the shape of that sword.</p></aside>
    </section>

