    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kathmandu lies in the north-western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, north of the Bagmati River, at an elevation of about 1,324 metres. Excavations at Hadigaon and Lubhu in the south of the valley found brick walls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> tools, and explorations indicate that Kathmandu and the two other sister towns of the valley were its oldest towns, traced to the period between 167 BCE and 1 CE. At Dhando Chaitya a brick with an inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> was found, which archaeologists believe to be two thousand years old. The oldest dated find is a life-size standing figure in the Kushan style, found at Maligaon in 1992 and dated by its inscription to samvat 107, most likely 185 CE. It is the oldest known inscription from the valley.</p><p>According to the Swayambhu Purana, the valley was once a deep lake where serpents lived and where Buddhas came to bathe. Vipassi Buddha sowed a lotus seed in it, and the bodhisattva Manjushri drained the lake by cutting the mountain with his sword, then founded a city called Manjupattan and made Dharmakar its ruler. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> Purana names a city Nayapala, from which the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> probably comes. Of the time before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a>, the genealogy called the Gopalraj Vansawali lists rulers of several lines, among them the Kirata dynasty founded by Yalamber. Two settlements stood on the site, Yambu in the northern half of the old city and Yengal in the southern half.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunakamadeva">Gunakamadeva</a> joined Yambu and Yengal, then called Koligram and Dakshin Koligram, and so founded the city. It was laid out in the shape of Chandrahrasa, the sword of Manjushri, and was surrounded by eight barracks guarded by the Ajimas, mother goddesses. According to the history of Kathmandu, a marketplace was formed at the centre in a circular shape and a temple to Bhimsen, god of merchants, was built to attract traders. The city stood on the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, which led to great growth in architecture and to the spread of Newar art, and Newar artists travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> making religious art.</p><p>In the Malla period the city again became prominent and for most of the period dominated the trade between India and Tibet, and Nepali currency became the standard currency of trans-Himalayan trade. Kathmandu was one of four fortified cities of the valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipur">Kirtipur</a>, which competed with one another in the arts, architecture and trade. The kings had public buildings, squares and temples built, developed water spouts, set up trusts called guthis, codified laws and had plays performed in city squares. The Kasthamandap, the wooden pavilion that gave the city its name, was rebuilt in 1596 under Lakshmi Narasimha Malla. A Capuchin missionary, Father Giuseppe, observed in the 1760s that the city contained about 18,000 houses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal.jpg" alt="Birendra of Nepal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">Birendra of Nepal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birendra_Bir_Bikram_Shah_c._1967_(restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CAPTAIN MEDUSA, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The rulers before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> are known only from traditional lists, which name Gopalas, Mahispalas, Abhirs, Kirat and Somavanshi. The Licchavis, who came from Vaisali in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, defeated the Kirats and established their dynasty around 400. They ruled from 400 to 750, and the 7th-century king Amshuverma is named among them. The period from 750 to 1200 is considered a transitional one, for which no concrete verifiable records are available. Arimalla, the first Malla king, ruled in the valley from 1200 to 1216, and Yakshamalla reigned from 1428 to 1482, at the height of the united Malla kingdom. In 1484 that kingdom divided into three, Kathmandu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhadgaon</a> and Patan.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha, who had become king in 1743, was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipur">Kirtipur</a> at first, but the Gorkha Kingdom ended the Malla confederation after the Battle of Kathmandu in 1768 and adopted Kathmandu as its capital. In the war of 1814 to 1816 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> lost territory by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a> of 4 March 1816, and Kathmandu had to accept a British Resident. Jang Bahadur began Rana rule in 1846, and Rana prime ministers governed until 1950. King Tribhuvan was restored in 1951. In 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendra-of-nepal">King Mahendra</a> abolished multiparty democracy and introduced the panchayat system, and on 9 April 1990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> restored it. After protests in 2006 King Gyanendra restored parliament on 24 April, and on 28 May 2008 the Constituent Assembly voted 560 to 4 to abolish the monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple.jpg" alt="Pashupatinath Temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple">Pashupatinath Temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pashupatinath_Temple-2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bijay Chaurasia, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kathmandu was the city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newars</a>, the indigenous inhabitants of the valley, who called it Yen Dey. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> both took root there. Buddhist monks are said to have established a forest monastery at Sankhu, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> period monasteries and orders were created that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-buddhism">Newar Buddhism</a>, which is still practised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> as its liturgical language. Hinduism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system are assumed to have been established in the valley together with the Licchavi kingdom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple">Pashupatinath Temple</a>, on the Bagmati, is the oldest Hindu temple in Kathmandu, and Hindus are cremated on the banks of the river, which Hindus and Buddhists both hold holy. Kathmandu also has the tradition of the Kumari, a young girl worshipped as the incarnation of the goddess Taleju, who lives in the Kumari Ghar beside Durbar Square.</p><p>The Newars were traders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> Newar merchants of Kathmandu carried art styles and Buddhism across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>. Other traditional occupations were farming, metal casting, woodcarving, painting, weaving and pottery. The old city had a Durbar Square with two quadrangles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a> palace in the inner one, and water came from stone spouts called dhunge dharas. The royal family lived in the Hanuman Dhoka until 1886, when it moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a>. In the Rana period the first modern school, Durbar High School, the first college, Trichandra College, and the first hospital, Bir Hospital, were built in the city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011 Nepali was the mother tongue of 62 per cent of the people and Newari of 19 per cent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka.jpg" alt="Hanuman Dhoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanuman_Dhoka_Durbar_Square_Kathmandu_(2023)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chainwit., CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early Malla years were turbulent, with raids and attacks by Khas and Turk Muslims. A devastating earthquake killed a third of the city's population, among them King Abhaya Malla. Together these disasters destroyed most of the architecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> period, such as the Managriha and Kailashkut Bhawan, and the literature kept in its monasteries was lost. In 1312 the Khasa king Ripumalla led a raid into the valley, and in 1345-46 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Shams ud-din Ilyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> invaded the city and destroyed many earlier buildings, which led to widespread reconstruction. A significant part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple">Pashupatinath Temple</a> was destroyed by invaders in the 14th century, and the temple seen now was built in the 19th century.</p><p>In 1767 the valley kings asked the British for help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gorkhas</a>, and a force of 2,500 under Captain Kinloch was overpowered. Rana rule began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-massacre">Kot massacre</a> of 1846 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jang Bahadur Rana</a> and his supporters killed most of the high officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and the Bhandarkhal massacre followed. The earthquake of 1934 damaged many parts of the city, which were later rebuilt. On 1 June 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipendra-of-nepal">Crown Prince Dipendra</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aishwarya-of-nepal">Queen Aishwarya</a> and others at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a>. On 25 April 2015 an earthquake of magnitude 7.8 killed 8,856 people in Nepal. In Kathmandu the Dharahara tower collapsed, killing 132 people, and Durbar Square was severely damaged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-malla.jpg" alt="Pratap Malla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla">Pratap Malla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Pratap_Malla_at_prayer.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kathmandu's name comes from the Kasthamandap, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words for wood and pavilion, and in the Malla period the city was sometimes called Kantipur, said to mean city of light. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> dated as late as the 20th century call it Kasthamandap Mahanagar, the great city. The trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> brought together artistic and architectural traditions from other cultures with local art, and the monuments were shaped over centuries by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist practice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has declared seven monument zones of the valley a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, inscribed in 1979 and modified in 2006: the Durbar squares of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a>, Patan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, the Hindu temples of Pashupatinath and Changunarayan and the Buddhist stupas of Swayambhunath and Boudhanath.</p><p>The Durbar Square of Kathmandu has about 50 temples, and the eastern wing of the Hanuman Dhoka, with ten courtyards, is the oldest part, dating to the mid-16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla">Pratap Malla</a> expanded it in the 17th century and built Rani Pokhari in 1670. Swayambhunath, on a hill in the north-west of the city, is among the oldest religious sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and is revered by Buddhists and Hindus alike. Tibetan refugees have built more than fifty Tibetan monasteries near Boudhanath. The place is now the capital and largest city of Nepal, with 845,767 people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2021.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kathmandu, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, grew from the Newar settlements of Yambu and Yengal and prospered on the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. It was a Malla royal city until the Gorkha conquest of 1768, then the capital of Nepal under Shah and Rana rule, and today is the country's capital and largest city.</em></p>

